        {
          "id": 92,
          "source": "kantei-funka",
          "score": 0.0328,
          "cos": 0.782
        }
      ],
      "a": "Touching volcanic ash can cause skin inflammation, such as pain, swelling, and infections from scratches [1]. Volcanic ash is very small and goes deep into the lungs with the air, which can increase coughing, make breathing difficult, increase runny nose or phlegm, and cause a sore nose or throat [2]. People with asthma or bronchitis may experience coughing fits, chest tightness, and labored breathing, and those with serious heart conditions should also be careful [2]. If volcanic ash gets into your eyes, do not rub it with your hands; rinse it with water and wear glasses instead of contact lenses [2]. Volcanic ash can cause a feeling of grittiness in the eyes, itching, pain, and redness. It can also cause conjunctivitis, making things feel scratchy or blurry [2].",
